      {
        "review_id": 125819922,
        "review_link": "https://www.tripadvisor.com/ShowUserReviews-g293890-d310712-r125819922-Pashupatinath_Temple-Kathmandu_Kathmandu_Valley_Bagmati_Zone_Central_Region.html",
        "title": "Truly incredible place! One of the 'must see' places in the world.",
        "text": "Being very honest, I didn't expect much from this visit. \"Yeah, another temple\" I thought. I was soooo wrong! Pashupatinath complex is truly amazing, fascinating and spiritual. \n\nTemple complex is located on the way to the airport, just few km from Thamel. There are plenty of buses and taxis around, but probably your driver would decide to wait for you. We did that and that was mistake - you should take your time and do not rush. Pashupatinath is quite large and there are so many things to see and experience. Entry fee for foreigners is 500 NRP (approx. 4,6 EUR).\n\nAt the entrance (like everywhere in Nepal) there are \"local guides\" which can help you. I usually refuse to use their services, but this time somehow happened that I didn't... That was absolutely the best choice I could do. Our guy, Prabin spent with us almost 2hours explaining in perfect English all sights, traditions, rituals and habits. It was stunning and I couldn't ask for more. Plus his sense of humour and professional approach and knowledge made our day. If you decide to go there, I can provide you his contact phone number and he could guide you around for some acceptable amount of money (not set, but you can agree before). \n\nGoing back to the temple it was really touchy to see how bodies are prepared and later burned on the Ghats... You can see that from the distance or just from above. I know that this may be sad occasion, but as far as I was advised tourists do not interrupt in these special private moments. It is up to you if you wish to take photos or not. It is definitely something different. Prabin explained differences between ceremonies and that was really fascinating.\n\nMain temple, Pashupati Mandir is not accessible for non-Hindu. I really didn't care that I wasn't able to visit main shrine (as I am not Hindu) - you have to accept that. All area and other accessible parts are worth visiting.\n\nGo there in the morning as this is less crowded and everything is just waking up. Incredible feeling. You're surrounded by hundreds of pilgrims, visitors, animals, Sadhus (holy-men or just \"commercial holy men\" as well)... I know that smell and dirt (“holy garbage in the Bagmati River) may impact your personal impression, but I was stunned with this place. Next time when in Kathmandu will spend there definitely few more hours...\n\nI only regret that we didn't have more time - 2h was far too short to see everything. Thanks to our guide we could actually see and understand many of the places. If you are interested to get his contact, please message me.",
